{"schema_version":1,"snapshot":"2021-12-03","source":{"repository":"matthiaskoenig/pkdb_analysis","path":"tests/data/testdata_concise_false.zip","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/matthiaskoenig/pkdb_analysis/develop/tests/data/testdata_concise_false.zip"},"sid":"PKDB00304","namespace":"pkdb","name":"Ghoneim1981","licence":"closed","access":"public","date":"2020-06-30","creator":"dannythekey","curators":["dannythekey","mkoenig"],"substances":["diazepam","nordazepam"],"identifiers":{"source_study_sid":"PKDB00304","pmid":"7226707","pmcid":null,"doi":null,"pkdb_sid":"PKDB00304"},"reference":{"title":"Diazepam effects and kinetics in Caucasians and Orientals.","date":"1981-07-23","journal":"Clinical pharmacology and therapeutics","authors":["M M Ghoneim","K Korttila","C K Chiang","L Jacobs","R D Schoenwald","S P Mewaldt","K O Kayaba"],"abstract":"Mental and psychomotor effects and diazepam kinetics were studied in Caucasian and Orientals. 12 Caucasian and 13 Oriental young adults received on one of two occasions, separated by 2 weeks, either 0.2-mg/kg diazepam or saline intravenously. Serum diazepam and desmethyldiazepam concentrations were measured by electron-capture gas-liquid chromatography in samples drawn up to 72 hr after injection. Serum protein binding was measured by equilibrium dialysis. Subjects were tested on a battery of psychological tests before and 0.5, 2, and 4 hr after treatment. While the free fraction of diazepam was identical in both races (0.02), volume of distribution at steady state (Vdss) was different when calculated as absolute volume (Vdss = 76.55 +/- 9.63 l in Caucasians and 54.96 +/- 4.55 l in Orientals, p = 0.04) and marginally significant when corrected for body weight (Vdssl/kg = 1.10 +/- 0.11 in Caucasian and 0.88 +/- 0.05 in Orientals, p = 0.07). total body clearance (Cl), but not elimination half-life (t 1/2), was higher in Caucasians than Orientals, p less than 0.01; t 1/2 = 37.70 +2- 5.53 hr in Caucasians and 41.77 +/- 3.80 in Orientals). Desmethyldiazepam levels were higher in Orientals than Caucasians. Mental and psychomotor effects were maximal at the first session (0.5 hr), followed by complete recovery by the 4-hr session. Effects were similar in both groups. If repeated dosing causes a higher rate of cumulated diazepam serum levels in Orientals, as expected, there might be deeper brain depression in that group."},"row_counts":{"groups":40,"individuals":0,"interventions":1,"outputs":60,"timecourses":4,"scatters":0},"entity_counts":{"groups":3,"individuals":0,"interventions":1,"outputs":60,"timecourses":4,"scatters":0},"groups":[{"study_name":"Ghoneim1981","measurement_type":"healthy","group_count":"25","group_name":"all","max":null,"substance":null,"count":25,"calculation_type":"sample mean","group_parent_pk":null,"sd":null,"unit":null,"se":null,"min":null,"cv":null,"median":null,"group_pk":1273,"characteristica_pk":26882,"mean":null,"choice":"Y","value":null},{"study_name":"Ghoneim1981","measurement_type":"species","group_count":"25","group_name":"all","max":null,"substance":null,"count":25,"calculation_type":"sample mean","group_parent_pk":null,"sd":null,"unit":null,"se":null,"min":null,"cv":null,"median":null,"group_pk":1273,"characteristica_pk":26883,"mean":null,"choice":"homo sapiens","value":null},{"study_name":"Ghoneim1981","measurement_type":"sex","group_count":"25","group_name":"all","max":null,"substance":null,"count":17,"calculation_type":"sample mean","group_parent_pk":null,"sd":null,"unit":null,"se":null,"min":null,"cv":null,"median":null,"group_pk":1273,"characteristica_pk":26884,"mean":null,"choice":"M","value":null},{"study_name":"Ghoneim1981","measurement_type":"sex","group_count":"25","group_name":"all","max":null,"substance":null,"count":8,"calculation_type":"sample mean","group_parent_pk":null,"sd":null,"unit":null,"se":null,"min":null,"cv":null,"median":null,"group_pk":1273,"characteristica_pk":26885,"mean":null,"choice":"F","value":null},{"study_name":"Ghoneim